# Brief — round 1

## What the input asks (quoted, `producer`#1 "Game objective")

> 复刻经典版本的超级马里奥 1，做成网页游戏

("Recreate the classic version of Super Mario Bros. 1 as a web game.") No other material is supplied.

## What that means for this round

A browser game that a player who knows the 1985 NES *Super Mario Bros.* recognises as that game and that plays like it.
It opens from a static `index.html` in a current desktop browser (Chrome/Firefox), with no server-side code and no
install step, keyboard controls, and runs offline from the delivered files (no CDN needed at play time).

### Priorities (in order)

1. **P1 — World 1-1, complete and faithful.** This is the bar for any release.
   - Layout of 1-1 as in the original: ground with its gaps, `?` blocks and bricks in their places (including the
     hidden 1-up block and the multi-coin brick), pipes (including the enterable pipe to the underground coin room and
     the exit pipe back), the stair blocks, flagpole and castle.
   - Mario's feel: walk/run (B) with momentum and skid, variable-height jump (A) depending on hold time and run speed,
     air control, camera that only scrolls forward (no going back left past the screen edge).
   - Enemies: Goomba (stomp flattens), Koopa Troopa (stomp → shell, kick shell, shell kills enemies), enemies fall into
     pits, turn at walls; touching an enemy hurts Mario.
   - Power-ups: Super Mushroom (small → big, big can break bricks), Fire Flower (fireballs that bounce), Starman
     (invincibility), 1-up mushroom; damage shrinks big Mario with brief invulnerability; small Mario dies.
   - Coins (blocks and floating), coin counter with 100 coins = 1-up.
   - HUD as in the original: MARIO score, coins, WORLD 1-1, TIME countdown; time-out kills Mario.
   - Lives (start with 3), death animation, falling in a pit kills, game over screen, title screen with start.
   - Flagpole end: height-based score, slide, walk into castle, remaining time converted to score.
   - Sound effects and music for the events above (jump, coin, stomp, bump, break, power-up appear/get, 1-up, pipe,
     fireball, death, flagpole, course clear, game over, overworld + underground themes, hurry-up at low time).
2. **P2 — the rest of World 1** (1-2 underground with warp zone, 1-3 athletic/treetops with moving lifts, 1-4 castle
   with firebars, lava, Bowser on the bridge, axe, Toad). Piranha Plants in pipes from 1-2 on.
3. **P3 — further worlds** (2-1 onward), only after P1 and P2 are solid. Not required for this round's release.

### Standard every part is judged against

- **Recognisable**: screens, level layout, enemies, HUD and sounds read as SMB1 at a glance and by ear; NES look
  (256×240 logical resolution, 16×16 tiles, integer-scaled, pixel-crisp, NES palette feel).
- **Plays right**: controls responsive at 60 fps logic; no falling through floors, no getting stuck in walls, no
  softlocks; every P1 mechanic above works on the delivered build in a real browser.
- **Complete for its priority**: a released level is playable start to flag (or axe) and to game over / restart.
- **Assets are original recreations** made for this game in the SMB1 style (pixel art and chiptune audio made or
  generated by the art position), not ripped ROM graphics or recorded Nintendo audio, so that the delivery can be
  handed over cleanly. Faithfulness is to look and feel, not to copied data.

### Controls (default)

Arrows = move, Down = crouch/enter pipe; `Z` or `J` = B (run / fireball); `X`, `K` or Space = A (jump);
Enter = Start/pause. (Equivalents are fine if documented on the title screen or in the README.)

## Decisions taken (round 1)

- **Scope:** World 1 (1-1 to 1-4) is the round's target; 1-1 alone is the minimum for a release. Art draws the World 1
  set: overworld, underground, athletic (treetops, lifts, red Koopa Paratroopa / Troopa) and castle (grey bricks, lava,
  firebar, Bowser, bridge, axe, Toad), plus Piranha Plant. Enemies of later
  worlds (Hammer Bros, Lakitu/Spiny, Cheep-cheep, Bloober, Buzzy Beetle, Bullet Bill) are not needed this round.
- **Originality:** all graphics drawn and all audio synthesised by us in SMB1's look and sound (NES palette, 16×16
  tiles, same silhouettes and proportions; pulse/triangle/noise voices, the same kind of SFX per event). No ROM-ripped
  graphics and no note-for-note transcription of the original themes: music is original chiptune in the same style,
  tempo feel and role (overworld, underground, castle, star, hurry-up, course clear, death, game over).
- **Reports** (qa checks, playtests) are working material that stays in their owners' repositories and reaches the
  producer through issues and releases; they are not part of the delivered game, so they get no delivered path.

## What I could not establish

- Whether the user wants exact original Nintendo graphics/audio. I set original recreations in the same style (above);
  if the user asks for the original melodies, that changes the music only.
- How far beyond World 1 the user expects. I set World 1-1 as the release bar, 1-2…1-4 next, more worlds later.
- Target devices: desktop keyboard first; touch controls are not required this round.

## How work flows this round

- `game-developer` builds the game in its repository (root, `index.html`, `src/`) and publishes each playable candidate as
  a release with a self-contained zip (game + current `assets/`) that runs by opening `index.html` or a static server.
- `artist` delivers everything under `assets/`, to the manifest of files, sizes, frames and formats the game loads.
  Build and art settle that manifest directly between them (`game-developer` issue #3 / #4).
- `producer` republishes each candidate as a release in the `producer` repository (tag `build-N`) with what to play,
  so `playtester` can download and play it; `qa` checks the same candidate against this brief.
- Defects go to the owner of the affected part; the producer accepts only a candidate qa has checked and the
  playtester has played and reported on.
